        "result": "Seasonally adjusted total nonfarm first-print changes, in thousands: April 115, May 172, June 57. Sources: [April release](https://www.bls.gov/news.release/archives/empsit_05082026.pdf), [May release](https://www.bls.gov/news.release/archives/empsit_06052026.pdf), [June release](https://www.bls.gov/news.release/archives/empsit_07022026.htm)."
      },
      {
        "kind": "tool",
        "tool": "official.lookup",
        "call": "Fetch the original BLS releases for July, August, and September 2026.",
        "result": "Seasonally adjusted total nonfarm first-print changes, in thousands: July -23, August 162, September 29. Sources: [July release](https://www.bls.gov/news.release/archives/empsit_08072026.htm), [August release](https://www.bls.gov/news.release/archives/empsit_09042026.htm), [September release](https://www.bls.gov/news.release/archives/empsit_10022026.htm)."
      },
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "The outside-view base rate is the mean of the six April-September first prints: (115 + 172 + 57 - 23 + 162 + 29)/6 = 85.333 thousand. This reference class matches the forecast's first-print measurement and includes substantial monthly volatility."
      },
      {
        "kind": "tool",
        "tool": "official.lookup",
        "call": "Fetch September's BLS Employment Situation summary and seasonally adjusted Table B-1.",
        "result": "September's first-print gain was 29 thousand versus a reported prior-12-month average gain of 45 thousand. July was revised from 21 to -10 thousand and August from 162 to 133 thousand, a combined downward revision of 60 thousand. Table B-1 shows August and September seasonally adjusted levels of 159015 and 159044 thousand. Sources: [BLS summary](https://www.bls.gov/news.release/empsit.nr0.htm), [Table B-1](https://www.bls.gov/news.release/empsit.t17.htm)."
      },
      {
        "kind": "tool",
        "tool": "official.lookup",
        "call": "Fetch official August JOLTS and the October 1 unemployment-insurance claims release.",
        "result": "August seasonally adjusted job openings were 7079 thousand versus July's revised 7335 thousand; hires were 5192 versus 5146 thousand. Layoffs and discharges declined to 1641 from 1702 thousand. The October 1 DOL release reported seasonally adjusted initial claims of 197000 for the week ending September 26 and a four-week average of 200000. Sources: [BLS JOLTS](https://www.bls.gov/news.release/jolts.htm), [DOL claims](https://www.dol.gov/sites/dolgov/files/OPA/newsreleases/ui-claims/20261543.pdf)."
      },
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "Level: employment stocks define the resolver; the forecast models their monthly change. Momentum: September's slower growth and falling vacancies favor a downward update from the base rate, while contained layoffs and claims support modest gains. One-off: education and hospitality volatility remains in the dispersion estimate; no separately quantified October adjustment is established. Policy mechanism: the fetched evidence provides no quantified October policy effect, so that adjustment is zero. These are forecasting judgments rather than fetched statistics."
      },
      {
        "kind": "math",
        "text": "Prior/update/interval: Use a mean-reverting persistence model with equal judgmental weights on the April-September first-print mean and September's first print. Prior = 85.333; momentum adjustment = 0.5*(29 - 85.333) = -28.167; separate one-off and policy adjustments = 0. Point = 57.167, rounded to 57 thousand. For the change-series values themselves, sum of squared deviations = 29981.333; sigma = 77.436 thousand = sqrt(29981.333/(6 - 1)). Normal-approximation 80% half-width = 1.28*sigma = 99.118. Apply a modest small-sample allowance of sqrt(1 + 1/6), giving half-width 107.059. Centering on 57 gives [-50.059, 164.059]; outward whole-thousand rounding gives final bounds [-51, 165]. This is an approximate predictive interval, not an interval for the historical mean."
      },
